<title>Image.load Function</title>


<subheader>Format</subheader>

<logicsyntax>
b = image_name.load(<arg>image_file_path</arg>);

b = image_name.load(<arg>valueset_name</arg>, <arg>value</arg>);
</logicsyntax>


<subheader>Description</subheader>

The <logiccolor>Image.load</logiccolor> function reads an image from a file and stores its contents in the <link Image.csdoc>Image</link> object. In the first version of the function, the string expression <arg>image_file_path</arg> specifies the location of the image on the disk.

The second version simplifies loading <link value_set_images.csdoc>value set images</link>. It is equivalent to calling:

<logicsyntax Image>
image_name.load(getimage(<arg>valueset_name</arg>, <arg>value</arg>));
</logicsyntax>

The following types of images are supported:

<list>
    <li><b>JPEG</b> (.jpg/.jpeg).</li>
    <li><b>PNG</b> (.png).</li>
    <li><b>BMP</b> (.bmp).</li>
    <li><b>ICO</b> (.ico). ICO files can only be loaded on Windows, but can be <link Image_save_function.csdoc>saved</link> on all platforms.</li>
    <li><b>GIF</b> (.gif). GIF files can only be loaded and cannot be <link Image_save_function.csdoc>saved</link>.</li>
</list>


<subheader>Return Value</subheader>

The function returns a logical value of 1 (<logiccolor>true</logiccolor>) if the image was loaded and 0 (<logiccolor>false</logiccolor>) if the image could not be loaded.


<subheader>Example</subheader>

<logic>
<include include_image_example_thumbnails.hgi />
</logic>


